around five thousand migrants have  entered spain's  north african enclave of ceuta some  swimming almost two kilometers along the  coastline from neighboring morocco  one man drowned in the crossing during a  record one day influx of people to the  territory  is one of two populated spanish enclaves  on  mainland africa authorities are sending  security reinforcements to the area amid  worsening relations between  spain and morocco  it started in the early hours of the  morning  groups of young men made the risky swim  from morocco to the neighboring spanish  territory of ceyuta  exhausted they then reported to  authorities for processing  as the day went on the tide dropped  exposing an  easy path around the border wall that  allowed whole families and miners to try  the crossing  some even resorting to an inflatable  boat  it's the biggest arrival of migrants  ever seen in ceuta  more than a thousand children were said  to be among the new arrivals  authorities are overwhelmed by the  influx  madrid says they are urgently sending  more resources to the region  many of the moroccan migrants celebrated  making it across the border  hoping to start a better life inside the  boundaries of the european union  but there's a high chance those  celebrations will be short-lived  spain's foreign minister says  authorities have already started sending  people back to morocco  it follows the arrival of more than a  hundred migrants in april  everyone except unaccompanied minors  were deported  you
 

